# `make` is copied in line, and in the copy the constructor of P: two frames below the loop. The constructor's stores ask whether a setter lies above (`AreMembersUnchanged [MemberPassedByStores]`); the rare side is a
# generic store, so the fresh object leaves and is built in every turn. The purchase of a second build of the loop looked for such guards in the text of the loop's own body alone (a read off a `new` with a proven callee).
# A try of the loop notes the guards of stores that it built, in whatever frame, and the purchase asks them once in front: in the typed build the object is never made.
# RUN: walk f2:Body mechanisms=all
# The bound is written and the sum is whole: nothing else is tested in front, so without the guard the loop is built once, with the guard and the birth in its turns.
# CHECK: isLoopHead=true
# CHECK: = NewObject
# CHECK: isLoopHead=true
# CHECK-NOT: = NewObject
# CHECK-NOT: = AreMembersUnchanged
# -- source: the-guard-of-stores-that-a-constructor-copied-through-a-helper-asks-stands-in-front-of-the-loop.cjs
